```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is entirely on router1 — the team's hypothesis that router2 dropped
the peering is incorrect.

**Affected component:** router1's eth0 interface (the inter-router link,
193.10.11.1/24), which is the physical/L2 prerequisite for the eBGP session
between AS 1 and AS 2.

**Mechanism:** router1's eth0 was brought down at 2026-07-30 20:50:39 (FRR
confirmed: "Link ups: 0 / Link downs: 1"). Because the interface is down, the
kernel never installs the 193.10.11.0/24 connected route, so router1 has no
path to BGP neighbor 193.10.11.2. The BGP TCP session therefore cannot be
established, leaving it permanently in the "Active" state. With no peering,
router1 never receives the UPDATE advertising 200.1.1.0/24, so it has no
forwarding entry for pc2's subnet. Every packet from pc1 (195.11.14.2) toward
200.1.1.2 hits router1 with no matching route and is dropped with "Destination
Net Unreachable."

**Localization:** router1, interface eth0 (`<BROADCAST,MULTICAST>` — missing
UP/LOWER_UP flags). router2 is fully operational: both its interfaces are UP,
193.10.11.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24 are connected, and it has already learned
195.11.14.0/24 via BGP.

**Root cause:** router1's eth0 interface was administratively shut down
(injected `ip link set eth0 down`), severing the only path to router2 and
collapsing the eBGP session.

**Corrective fix:** On router1, run `ip link set eth0 up`. The connected route
193.10.11.0/24 will immediately return to the RIB, BGP will re-establish its
TCP session to 193.10.11.2, and within seconds router1 will receive and install
the BGP route to 200.1.1.0/24, restoring full pc1 ↔ pc2 connectivity. No
action on router2 is required.
=== END ===
```